cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this dataset or code, please cite the JCDL 2026 paper (preferred-citation) and this dataset."
type: dataset
title: "Replication Data for: Same Problem, Different Field - Cross-Domain Solution Import via Domain-Stripped Computational Fingerprints"
authors:
  - family-names: Kulikowski
    given-names: Eryk
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9967-9732"
    affiliation: "LIBIS, KU Leuven"
    email: eryk.kulikowski@kuleuven.be
abstract: >-
  Reproduction package for the JCDL 2026 paper. The same underlying computational
  problem is re-solved across unrelated fields under different names; topical and
  citation-based scientific embeddings cannot see this kinship. Each paper is reduced
  to a domain-stripped, method-name-stripped computational fingerprint (one cached LLM
  call per paper) and cross-field twins are ranked with a cheap embedder over those
  fingerprints. The package contains the curated benchmark (109 papers, 18 method
  families, 210 twin pairs), the 501-paper wild corpus as fingerprints, the per-paper
  provenance manifest, versioned link-lists and checksums to rebuild the full-text
  corpus from open sources, abstracts where licensed (arXiv CC0 metadata, PubMed
  Central CC BY), the distillation prompts, the evaluation code, the four executed
  cross-domain imports, and the model identifiers, prompt versions and run dates per
  distiller and annotator arm. Every reported number is regenerated by a shipped script.
  The headline rows and the four executed imports run offline from the bundled
  fingerprints; the abstract baselines require one online rebuild of five
  non-redistributable abstracts (checksum-verified), and the embedder baselines
  download their public checkpoints on first run.
keywords:
  - scientific information retrieval
  - cross-domain discovery
  - solution import
  - computational isomorphism
  - faceted representation
  - LLM distillation
  - digital libraries
  - benchmark
